            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"Eternal life, spiritual vitality, and the person of Jesus Christ Himself as the source of divine life and salvation (often distinguishing the Greek 'zoe' from mere physical 'bios').\", \"primary_basis\": [\"John 14:6\", \"John 1:4\", \"John 11:25\", \"1 John 5:11-12\", \"John 10:10\"], \"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"Physical existence, breath, or earthly duration.\", \"basis\": [\"Genesis 2:7\", \"James 4:14\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}, {\"meaning\": \"The Holy Spirit's animating and regenerating power.\", \"basis\": [\"Romans 8:2\", \"John 6:63\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}, {\"meaning\": \"The soul or vital animating force residing in the blood, tied to atonement.\", \"basis\": [\"Leviticus 17:11\", \"Deuteronomy 12:23\"], \"prominence\": \"minority\"}], \"has_settled_consensus\": true, \"confidence\": \"high\", \"notes\": \"In biblical theology, particularly in the New Testament and overwhelmingly in Johannine literature, 'the life' symbolizes eternal, divine existence and fellowship with God. Christ is frequently identified not just as the giver of life, but as the personification of 'the life' itself.\"}",
